---
import '../../styles/global.css';
import MemoryCalculator from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/MemoryCalculator.tsx';
import IntBits from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/IntBits.tsx';
import FloatAnatomy from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/FloatAnatomy.tsx';
import RangeVsPrecision, { FormatTable } from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/RangeVsPrecision.tsx';
import Quiz from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/Quiz.tsx';
import { Bite } from '../../components/artifacts/number-formats/ui.tsx';

import Sources from '../../components/Sources.astro';

const sources = [
  {
    title: 'A Visual Guide to Quantization',
    url: 'https://newsletter.maartengrootendorst.com/p/a-visual-guide-to-quantization',
    author: 'Maarten Grootendorst',
    note: 'The main reference for this series. This page is my own notes and examples built after reading it.',
  },
];

const title = 'Number formats: FP32, FP16, BF16 and INT8';
const description = 'A small, interactive guide to how computers store numbers, and why it matters for model size.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site)} />
    <title>{title}</title>
    <meta name="description" content={description} />
    <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
    <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-white text-neutral-900 antialiased">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-5 py-12 sm:py-20">
      <nav class="text-sm text-neutral-500">
        <a href="/" class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-neutral-900">← Maula Irfani</a>
      </nav>

      <header class="mt-8">
        <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-widest text-neutral-400">Quantization · Part 1</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Number formats</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
          Before we can shrink a model, we need to know how it stores its numbers. This page is my set of small bites
          to learn that, with tiny examples you can play with. Click the bits. Break things.
        </p>
      </header>

      <Bite
        n={1}
        title="Why do we care?"
        remember="Memory = number of parameters × bytes per number. Fewer bytes per number means a smaller model."
        question="But what is inside those bytes? Let us open one up."
      >
        <p>
          A model is a huge pile of numbers called <b>parameters</b>. Every number takes some bytes of memory. So:
        </p>
        <p class="rounded-lg bg-neutral-100 px-4 py-3 text-center font-mono text-sm">
          memory = number of parameters × bytes per number
        </p>
        <p>
          Let us start small. Say each number takes <b>4 bytes</b> (this is the FP32 format, you will see why later).
          Now count up:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="w-full min-w-[22rem] text-left text-sm tabular-nums">
            <thead class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-neutral-400">
              <tr>
                <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">Numbers</th>
                <th class="py-2 pr-3 font-medium">× 4 bytes</th>
                <th class="py-2 font-medium">In plain words</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-neutral-200">
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">1</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-500">tiny</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">1,000</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4,000 bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-500">4 KB, a short text file</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">1 million</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 million bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-500">4 MB, one phone photo</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">1 billion</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">4 billion bytes</td><td class="py-2 text-neutral-500">4 GB, a small movie</td></tr>
              <tr class="font-medium"><td class="py-2 pr-3">70 billion</td><td class="py-2 pr-3">280 billion bytes</td><td class="py-2">280 GB</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Here is the shortcut. One billion bytes is one gigabyte (GB). So when the parameters are counted in
          billions, the answer is already in GB: <b>70 billion × 4 bytes = 280 GB</b>. Pick a model size below and
          watch what each format does.
        </p>
        <MemoryCalculator client:load />
        <p>Fewer bytes per number means a smaller model. So the goal is clear: use fewer bytes.</p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        n={2}
        title="Bits are switches"
        remember="N bits give 2^N patterns. 8 bits give 256 patterns, so INT8 holds 256 whole numbers."
        question="INT8 only holds whole numbers like 0 to 255. How do we store 0.15625, or a huge number like one billion?"
      >
        <p>
          A <b>bit</b> is a switch that is either 0 or 1. Put 8 of them in a row and you have a <b>byte</b>. Each
          switch is worth a different amount: 1, 2, 4, 8, and so on. Add up the switches that are on.
        </p>
        <IntBits client:load />
        <p>
          With 8 bits there are 2<sup>8</sup> = 256 patterns. That is why INT8 can hold only 256 different values.
          And they are whole numbers only.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        n={3}
        title="Floats: sign, exponent, mantissa"
        remember="A float is sign × (1 + mantissa) × 2^exponent. The exponent sets the scale. The mantissa sets the detail."
        question="FP16 and BF16 both use 16 bits, but they split them in different ways. Why have two, and what does each gain or lose?"
      >
        <p>
          The answer is <b>floating point</b>. It works like scientific notation. Take 0.15625 from the question above.
          We can write it as:
        </p>
        <p class="rounded-lg bg-neutral-100 px-4 py-3 text-center font-mono text-sm">
          0.15625 = +1 × 1.25 × 2<sup>-3</sup>
        </p>
        <p>
          Three pieces: a <b class="text-rose-700">sign</b> (+ or -), an <b class="text-amber-700">exponent</b> (the
          power of 2, which sets the scale), and a <b class="text-sky-700">mantissa</b> (the digits after the
          point). The leading 1 in 1.25 is always there, so we do not store it. It is called the hidden 1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exponent is stored with a <b>bias</b> so it can be negative. For FP32 the bias is 127. Click any bit
          below and watch the number change.
        </p>
        <FloatAnatomy client:load />
        <p>
          Now switch to FP16 and BF16 above. Same idea, but the bits are split in different ways. Look at how many
          bits the exponent and the mantissa get.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite
        n={4}
        title="Range vs precision"
        remember="More exponent bits mean more range. More mantissa bits mean more precision. With a fixed number of bits, you trade one for the other."
        question="Back to our memory problem: how far can we cut the bytes before the numbers break?"
      >
        <p>
          That split is the whole trade. More exponent bits give a wider <b>range</b>: how big and how small a number can be. More mantissa bits
          give more <b>precision</b>: how close neighbour numbers are. With a fixed number of bits, you trade one
          for the other.
        </p>
        <FormatTable />
        <p>
          FP16 and BF16 both use 16 bits, but they choose differently. FP16 keeps more precision. BF16 keeps the
          same range as FP32 and gives up precision. Type a number below and see what each format does to it. Try
          70000 and 0.00001.
        </p>
        <RangeVsPrecision client:load />
        <p>
          Notice that 0.1 is never exact in any format. Computers count in 2s, and 0.1 does not fit neatly. More
          mantissa bits just get closer.
        </p>
      </Bite>

      <Bite n={5} title="Quick quiz">
        <p>Six questions to check what stuck from the four bites above. Your score is not saved.</p>
        <Quiz client:load />
      </Bite>

      <p class="border-t border-neutral-200 pt-8 text-sm text-neutral-500">
        Next up, the answer to the last question: how to turn a big format into a small one (INT8, INT4) without
        losing too much.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8">
        <Sources items={sources} />
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
